oing on there but it's fun and  it's really really popular here in South  Korea so the arena is full of energy and  it's like a sport an event any a  sporting event  popular sport or method you'd you'd  you'd go to back home there's a lot of  energy and the crowds really into what  they're seeing so that makes it a lot of  fun and actually one last thing we saw  the women's u.s. hockey team win gold  today that was so there were so many  emotions in that arena with the  Canadians losing they were sad that was  it was hard to you know your heart went  out to them and then you know America  pulling through and and winning on a  shootout that was really dramatic and it  was just something witness I couldn't  believe that I actually was there to see  it in person you get recognized in South  Korea just by a couple people that are  there you know there's not that many  people over here to know who I am I'm  I'm afraid wait but that could be a good  thing admit yeah I mean I've it's it's  nice to be anonymous I suppose walking  down the street and yeah I mean it's uh  it's it's okay what's the sport would  you that you would like to try you might  be good at I think you know the one that  relates closely for me is bobsledding  when I was a little kid watching the  Olympics I always gravitated toward the  bobsled because it's a you know it's  mimic you're driving something you're in  a you're in a you're in some sort of  vehicle and I learned today that there's  a lot of comparisons to how they  actually train and have to set the sled  up I mean it's as important that they're  in shape and able to do what they do as  a team together it's as important that  the sled handles the track well and the  track  the track surface can be different and  that would require them to adjust and  tune the slid a lot of the guys that are  actually have crew chiefs and mechanics  so the crew chiefs actually have come  from NASCAR backgrounds one of the guys  that I met today worked at Pensk